.TH CC 1
.SH NAME
cc \- C compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cc
[\ \-cESOsv\ ] [\ \-Mc\ ] [\ \-o\ name\ ] [\ \-Dname[=value]\ ] [\ \-Uname\ ]
[\ \-Idir\ ] [\ \-F\ size\ ] file ... [\ \-lname\ ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cc
is the C compiler. It compiles C programs, and assembles assembly
language programs, into executable programs for XENIX on the 8086 and
8088, running each of the compiler's passes in turn: the preprocessor,
the compiler proper, the optimizer if asked for, the assembler
.BR as86 (1)
and the link editor
.BR ld86 (1).
.PP
Each
.I file
is taken according to its suffix:
.TP 10
.B .c
a C source program: preprocessed, compiled, and assembled into an
object file whose name is that of the source with
.B .o
in place of
.BR .c .
.TP
.B .s
an assembly language source program: assembled by
.BR as86 (1)
into a
.B .o
file. It is not preprocessed.
.TP
.B .o
an object file, from an earlier compilation: passed to the link.
.TP
.B .a
a library of object files, made with
.BR ar86 (1):
passed to the link, which takes from it the object files the program
needs.
.PP
Unless
.BR \-c ,
.B \-S
or
.B \-E
is given, the object files are then linked, with the start-up routine
.B /usr/lib/cc/crt0.o
first and the C library
.B /usr/lib/cc/libc.a
last, into an executable program named
.BR a.out .
When a single C program is compiled and linked by one command, its
.B .o
file is removed once the program is made. When more than one file is
named, cc prints the name of each source file as it compiles it.
.PP
The options are:
.TP 12
.B \-c
Compile and assemble only, leaving a
.B .o
file for each source file; do not link.
.TP
.B \-S
Compile only, leaving the assembly language for each C file in a file
with the suffix
.BR .s ;
do not assemble or link.
.TP
.B \-E
Run only the preprocessor on the C files, and send its output to the
standard output.
.TP
.BI \-o " name"
Name the executable program
.I name
instead of
.BR a.out .
cc refuses a
.I name
ending in .c or .s, which would overwrite a source file.
.TP
.B \-O
Optimize. The peephole optimizer rewrites the compiler's output by its
rules in /usr/lib/cc/rules.*, and the assembler makes each jump as short
as its distance allows. The program comes out smaller and faster.
.TP
.B \-Mc
Compile code in which a function keeps no registers for the function
that called it (the "caller saves" convention): smaller code again.
Compile all of a program's C files with
.BR \-Mc ,
or none of them: a function compiled without it expects the functions
it calls to keep its register variables, which code compiled with it
does not do. The libraries are compiled without it, and work with
either.
.B \-M0
and
.BR \-Ms ,
which name the small model, are accepted and change nothing; the small
model is the only one.
.TP
.B \-s
Leave the symbol table out of the executable program, as
.BR strip (1)
would. By default the symbol table is kept, for
.BR nm (1)
and the debugger
.BR adb (1).
.TP
.B \-v
Print each pass's command line before running it.
.TP
.BI \-D name
.PD 0
.TP
.BI \-D name = value
.PD
Define
.I name
to the preprocessor, as if by
.BR #define ,
with the value 1 if none is given.
.TP
.BI \-U name
Remove any initial definition of
.IR name .
.TP
.BI \-I dir
Look for
.B #include
files in the directory
.I dir
before looking in
.BR /usr/include .
A file named in quotes, as in #include "defs.h", is looked for first in
the directory of the file that includes it. Several
.B \-I
options are searched in the order given.
.TP
.BI \-F " size"
Give the program a stack of
.I size
bytes, written in hexadecimal, as with the cc of XENIX. The default is
1000 (4096 bytes). XENIX gives an 8086 program a stack of fixed size,
which it cannot grow while the program runs: a program with deep
recursion or large local arrays may need more.
.TP
.BI \-l name
Link with the library
.BI /usr/lib/cc/lib name .a\fR.\fP
The link takes from a library only the object files that the files
before it on the command line need, so put
.B \-l
after them.
.B \-lm
is the math library
.RB ( intro (3)),
.B \-ltermcap
the terminal capability library
.RB ( termcap (3)).
.PP
Other arguments beginning with \- are reported as unknown and ignored.
.SS "The Language"
cc compiles the C language described in
.I "The C Programming Language"
by Brian W. Kernighan and Dennis M. Ritchie (Prentice-Hall, 1978), with
the additions made to it since:
.B void
functions,
.B enum
types, structure assignment, and structures passed to and returned from
functions. It does not take the function prototypes or the
.B const
and
.B volatile
of the ANSI committee's draft standard. Functions are declared, and
their arguments given types, the original way:
.PP
.nf
	long
	sum(a, n)
	int *a;
	int n;
	{
		...
	}
.fi
.PP
A function that returns anything but an
.B int
must be declared before it is called, or the value is taken to be an
int: include the header for library functions, or declare them, as in
.BR "double atof();" .
.SS "The Machine"
Programs are compiled for the 8086's small model, with separate
instruction and data spaces: up to 64K bytes of code, and up to 64K of
data, stack and memory from
.BR malloc (3)
together. A
.B char
is 8 bits and unsigned (0 to 255), a
.B short
and an
.B int
16 bits, a
.B long
32 bits, and a pointer 16 bits.
.B float
and
.B double
are the IEEE single and double precision formats, 32 and 64 bits; the
arithmetic is done in software, correctly rounded, so no 8087 is needed
and a program gives the same results on every machine.
.PP
The preprocessor defines
.BR M_XENIX ,
.BR M_I86 ,
.BR M_I8086 ,
.B M_I86SM
and
.BR unix ,
as the cc of XENIX does, and
.BR __BCC__ ,
.B __8086__
and
.BR __BCC_VERSION__ .
.SS "Debugging"
A program keeps its symbol table unless it is linked with
.BR \-s ,
so the debugger
.BR adb (1),
from the Operating System's disk N5, knows its functions and variables
by name. The C names begin with an underscore:
.B main
is
.BR _main .
These are some of adb's commands:
.TP 16
.B adb prog
start adb on the program prog
.TP
.B _square:br
set a breakpoint at the function square
.TP
.B :r
run the program; arguments may follow
.TP
.B :co
continue after a breakpoint
.TP
.B :s
execute one instruction
.TP
.B $c
show the chain of function calls, with their arguments
.TP
.B _count/d
show the int variable count, in decimal
.TP
.B _main,8?ia
show the first eight instructions of main
.TP
.B :k
kill the program
.TP
.B $q
leave adb
.PP
A breakpoint stops a function at its first instruction, before it has
made its stack frame: execute two instructions
.RB ( :s
twice) before
.BR $c ,
to see the function itself in the chain of calls.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 10
.B CCROOT
A directory under which cc looks for its passes, libraries and headers,
instead of under /: the way to try a Development System that has not
been installed.
.SH FILES
.TP 26
file.c
C source
.TP
file.s
assembly language source
.TP
file.o
object file
.TP
a.out
the executable program
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/bcc-cpp
the preprocessor
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/bcc-cc1
the compiler proper
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/copt
the optimizer
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/rules.*
the optimizer's rules
.TP
/usr/bin/as86
the assembler
.TP
/usr/bin/ld86
the link editor
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/aout2x
turns the link editor's output into a XENIX executable
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/crt0.o
the start-up routine
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/libc.a
the C library
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/libm.a
the math library
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/libtermcap.a
the terminal capability library
.TP
/usr/include
the standard headers
.TP
/tmp/ctm*
temporary files
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each pass reports errors with the name of the file and the line. cc
exits with status 0 when every pass succeeded, and 8 when one failed or
the command line was wrong.
.SH SEE ALSO
as86(1), ld86(1), ar86(1), make(1), nm(1), size(1), strip(1), adb(1),
intro(3)
.PP
B. W. Kernighan and D. M. Ritchie,
.IR "The C Programming Language" ,
Prentice-Hall, 1978.
.PP
D. M. Ritchie,
.IR "C Reference Manual" ,
Bell Laboratories.
.SH HISTORY
A
.B cc
command first appeared in Version 2 AT&T UNIX, in 1972. The C language
was designed by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Laboratories, and grew out of
Ken Thompson's language B.
.PP
The options of this cc are those of the cc of Version 7 AT&T UNIX and
System V, with
.B \-F
from the cc of XENIX.
.SH AUTHORS
The compiler passes are those of BCC, written by Bruce Evans. The cc
command, the start-up routine and the C library were written for the
xenix86-devsys Development System.
.SH BUGS
The preprocessor writes floating point constants in hexadecimal for the
compiler, which has no room to convert decimal ones; the output of
.B \-E
shows them as they were written.
